## What this chart installs

The CustomResourceDefinitions the Kasm Agent operator owns, and nothing else:

| Name | Group | Kind |
| ---- | ----- | ---- |
| `agents.agent.kasm.com` | `agent.kasm.com` | `Agent` |
| `kasmworkspaces.agent.kasm.com` | `agent.kasm.com` | `KasmWorkspace` |
| `kasmimagepullers.agent.kasm.com` | `agent.kasm.com` | `KasmImagePuller` |

No ServiceAccount, no RBAC, no operator Deployment — those belong to
[`kasm-agent-operator`](../kasm-agent-operator). This chart has **no values**: the templates are
static manifests, byte for byte the schemas the operator repository generates, and there is nothing
about a CRD schema that a cluster operator should be dialling.

## Why this chart exists

Helm has two ways to install a CustomResourceDefinition, and each one gives up something the other
has:

* A chart's **`crds/` directory** is applied *before* the release manifest is rendered and validated.
  That is what makes a one-command install work when the same release also creates custom resources
  of those kinds — as the `kasm-agent` umbrella does, with its `Agent` and `KasmImagePuller`
  resources. The cost: `helm upgrade` **never touches** a `crds/` CRD. Schema changes have to be
  applied out of band, with `kubectl apply --server-side`.
* An ordinary **template** is owned by the release, so `helm upgrade` applies schema changes like any
  other resource. The cost: it is rendered and validated in the same pass as everything else, so it
  cannot supply the schema that same release's custom resources are validated against.

The repository ships both, and this chart is the second half:

| | `kasm-agent-operator`'s `crds/` | `kasm-agent-crds` (this chart) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Purpose | one-command install of the agent stack | Helm-native CRD lifecycle for a fleet |
| Installed by | `helm install` of any chart that includes the operator | its own `helm install` release |
| Upgraded by | nothing — `kubectl apply --server-side` out of band | `helm upgrade` of this release |
| Removed by | nothing (`helm uninstall` leaves them) | nothing (`helm.sh/resource-policy: keep`) |

Use this chart when a fleet's CRD versions should move through the same review, promotion, and
rollout machinery as everything else you deploy with Helm. Skip it — and let the operator chart's
`crds/` directory do the work — when a single cluster is installed once and upgraded by hand.

The `kubectl apply --server-side -f charts/kasm-agent-operator/crds/` side channel remains valid
either way, and stays the documented path for a cluster that is not running this release.

The two copies of each schema originate in the same place: `config/crd/bases/` in the
`kasm-agent-operator` repository, regenerated there with `make manifests`. `make crds-sync-check`
fails the build if the copies ever disagree, so re-copying into only one of them cannot slip through.

## Install order

On a fresh fleet, **this release goes in first** — before `kasm-agent` or `kasm-platform`:

```console
helm install kasm-agent-crds oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-agent-crds

helm install kasm-agent oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-agent -n kasm-agent --create-namespace -f values.yaml
```

The CRDs are cluster-scoped, so this release's namespace is immaterial; put it wherever your fleet
tooling keeps cluster-scoped releases.

The second command still carries the operator chart's `crds/` directory, which is harmless: Helm
skips a CRD that already exists rather than replacing it. If you would rather the app release never
reason about CRDs at all, add `--skip-crds` to it.

## Upgrade discipline

**Upgrade this release first, then the app releases.** A new operator expects its new schema; the
app release that carries it must land on a cluster whose CRDs already accept the fields it writes.

```console
helm upgrade kasm-agent-crds oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-agent-crds

helm upgrade kasm-agent oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-agent -n kasm-agent -f values.yaml
```

The reverse order leaves a window in which the new operator writes fields the stored schema prunes.

**Prefer rolling forward to rolling back.** `helm rollback` on this release will happily reinstate an
older schema *underneath objects that are already stored in the newer one* — the API server prunes
fields that the restored schema does not define, and the data is gone, silently and irreversibly.
There is no dry run that will warn you. If an upgrade of this release goes wrong, fix the schema and
upgrade again.

## The `keep` annotation

Every CRD in this chart carries `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep`. `helm uninstall` therefore leaves
all five definitions — and every custom resource stored in them: every `Agent`, every live
`KasmWorkspace` — in place. Uninstalling this release gives up Helm's ownership of the CRDs; it does
not delete anything.

Deleting a CRD is always a deliberate `kubectl` act, and it cascades to every custom resource of that
kind in every namespace:

```console
kubectl delete crd agents.agent.kasm.com   # deletes every Agent in the cluster
```

Delete the custom resources first and let the operator clear their finalizers, exactly as the
`kasm-agent` chart's uninstall procedure describes, before going anywhere near the definitions.

## Adopting CRDs that Helm did not install

A cluster that already ran `helm install` on `kasm-agent`, `kasm-platform`, or `kasm-agent-operator`
has these CRDs in place — but installed through the `crds/` mechanism, which attaches **no ownership
metadata**. Installing this chart on such a cluster fails Helm's ownership check:

```
Error: rendered manifests contain a resource that already exists. Unable to continue with install:
CustomResourceDefinition "agents.agent.kasm.com" in namespace "" exists and cannot be imported into
the current release: invalid ownership metadata; label validation error: missing key
"app.kubernetes.io/managed-by": must be set to "Helm"
```

Label and annotate the existing CRDs so Helm recognises them as belonging to this release, then
install as normal. Substitute your release name and namespace:

```console
for crd in agents.agent.kasm.com kasmimagepullers.agent.kasm.com kasmworkspaces.agent.kasm.com \
           warmpools.pools.kasm.ai warmpoolinstances.pools.kasm.ai; do
  kubectl label crd "$crd" app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=Helm --overwrite
  kubectl annotate crd "$crd" meta.helm.sh/release-name=<release> \
    meta.helm.sh/release-namespace=<namespace> --overwrite
done
```

```console
helm install <release> oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-agent-crds -n <namespace>
```

This is metadata only: no schema is rewritten, no stored object is touched, and no custom resource is
disturbed. The `<namespace>` in the annotation is the namespace of *this* release — the CRDs
themselves stay cluster-scoped — and it must match the `-n` of the install that follows, or the
ownership check fails again on the namespace instead of the label.

## Chart value settings

This chart deliberately has none. See [`values.yaml`](values.yaml) for why, and
[`charts/kasm-agent-operator`](../kasm-agent-operator) for everything about the operator that *is*
configurable.
